{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"1adc999d954d41c0","type":"text","text":"Les premiers ministres britanniques depuis 1945","x":-341,"y":-100,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"2e226710cbeb1390","type":"text","text":"Numerous significant measures ==were taken== during his premiership\n- In 1946 , he ==enforced== __the Beveridge report__ : They ==created== social assistance / unemployment benefits  + They ==created== the __National Health Service__ : Which made healthcare free for British citizens \n- The same year : They ==launched== __social security__ which protected workers when they could no longer work , ==provided== that they ==had paid== a contribution during the working weekend.  ","x":360,"y":-620,"width":502,"height":299},
		{"id":"e4bb6a253e0c8f3b","type":"text","text":"He ==improved== the care of orphans in 1948 + ==developed== the National Parks in 1949","x":650,"y":-293,"width":212,"height":117},
		{"id":"5af6719ffb7652f6","type":"text","text":"All of industry like coal industry , electricity services and transport systems ==were nationalised==","x":373,"y":-309,"width":238,"height":149},
		{"id":"d793c6fdcd2108cf","type":"text","text":"Decolonisation process : \n- He ==started== the decolonisation process => Several of the British colonies or territories ==were== granted independence \n- -> Jordan in 1946 / India and Pakistan in 1947\n\n","x":383,"y":-143,"width":457,"height":163},
		{"id":"07295ce46444b6a4","type":"text","text":"After the war , Russia ==had blockaded== rail , road and water access to the allied sectors of Berlin in June 1948. \n-> Depriving some __2.5 million__ of people of food , medicine , coal and basic goods.\nAtlee , together with Americans , ==sent== planes to airlift food and fuel to population \n-> It cost __$2.7 billions__ , the largest share of any European nation. \n","x":383,"y":80,"width":517,"height":240},
		{"id":"854810b2a4ae2cb1","type":"text","text":"Clement Attlee (1883-1967)","x":24,"y":-100,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"32c6928e6cfa47ca","type":"text","text":"Wiston Churchill (1874-1965)","x":-342,"y":63,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"d4d32242190f1703","type":"text","text":"He ==studied== at the Royal Military College of Standhurst.\nHe ==became== officier and ==took== part in several battles in India , the Sudan..\n__In 1900__ , he ==was elected== a Conservative MP , he ==joined== the liberal party __in 1904__. \nHe ==served== in the Cabinet as President of the Board of Trade , then as Home secretary. \n","x":10,"y":63,"width":350,"height":257},
		{"id":"67259ca7344e270b","type":"text","text":"- __In 1940__ , he ==succeeded== Neville Chamberlin as Prime Minister and ==formed== a national government. \n- He  ==had lost== the elections in 1945, then again in 1950, he came back office in 1951. \n- Between his terms , he ==wrote== several books relating to his journeys in Africa , his experience in India and during the war => He ==received== the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1953 \n- __April 5th 1955__ , he ==resigned== and ==was succeeded== by his Foreign Secretary = Anthony Eden ","x":-427,"y":240,"width":437,"height":300},
		{"id":"90d47b64ca2e64c4","type":"text","text":"During his second term as PM , several measures ==were taken== by his government : \n- ==Insisted== on house building -> the shortage of houses due to German air raids \n- Denationalisation of the steel industry \n- He ==phased-out== rationing wich ==had started== in 1940\n- ==Insisted== on the development of the British atomic bomb wich ==was started== in 1952","x":-860,"y":240,"width":400,"height":283},
		{"id":"bee0418d92dd3843","type":"text","text":"First deputy because the function ==had never existed== before","x":274,"y":-940,"width":262,"height":111},
		{"id":"7992162f0b544345","type":"text","text":"Clement Atlee ==had been appointed== by Churchill (was a labour )","x":-55,"y":-924,"width":240,"height":80},
		{"id":"9ceb3ac3797ad863","type":"text","text":"Decolonisation : \n-British colonie like Kenya , several uprising ==took== place which ==were== violently repressed.","x":-599,"y":580,"width":345,"height":120},
		{"id":"048d07a961bcfb11","type":"text","text":"He ==had studied== at Oxford University . \nHe ==was shocked== by the poverty in the East End of London , so he ==had joined== the labour party in 1908 .\nHe ==had been== an officier during the First World War ","x":-22,"y":-702,"width":342,"height":164},
		{"id":"ea3c39ce2462f4fc","type":"text","text":"Margaret Thatcher (1925-2013)","x":-1038,"y":-130,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4bd1932860e1ecc2","type":"text","text":"She ==studied== at Oxford University. \n- In 1953, she ==read== law and became a barrister\n- In 1959 , she ==was elected== MP -> Two years later , __Harold Macmillan__ ==appointed== her __Secretary to the Ministry for Pensions__\n- She ==became== the spokesperson in the Commons for the Conservative when the Conservative party ==lost== the election in 1964 => ==voted== in favour of __decriminalising homosexuality__ and __legalising abortion__.\n- From 1966 to 1970 , she ==was== a member of the Shadow Cabinet : as Shadow Minister for Fuel and Power / for Transport and Secretary of State of Education. ","x":-1410,"y":-10,"width":497,"height":360},
		{"id":"9f04455d502200a8","type":"text","text":" - In 1970 Edward Heath ==won== the election in 1970 , she ==was appointed== to the Cabinet as secretary of state for Education and Science \n- She ==became== Conservative Party Leader in 1975 => She ==wanted== to improve the country’s economy and control inflation, while keeping a fair balance between workers’ rights and duties. She also ==planned== to reward hard work, support families and education, protect those in real need, and make Britain stronger and safer with the help of its allies.","x":-1920,"y":-10,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"d0ae94e6119d3088","type":"text","text":"- After __Callaghan's defeat in 1979__, she ==became== the first ever female Prime Minister of the United Kingdom.  -> She ==remained== England's PM from May 4th 1979 to November 28th 1990 when she resigned","x":-1843,"y":382,"width":418,"height":135},
		{"id":"9a47099f428f51ef","type":"text","text":"- **1979:** She ==criticized== the Labour Party for too many taxes, too many rules, and helping weak industries. She ==wanted== a **free economy**.\n- **1979 (Dublin Summit):** Thatcher ==said== the UK paid too much to the EEC and ==demanded==, _“We want our money back.”_\n- **1980:** She ==began== the **“Right to Buy”** plan, letting people buy their home at a large discount, and created **\"enterprise zones\"**to attract businesses.\n- **1980:** During the **Iran Embassy siege**, she ==sent== the **SAS** to stop terrorists — it ==was== a success.\n- **1982:** She ==sent== the army to take back the **Falkland Islands** after Argentina ==invaded== — Britain ==won==, and she ==became== very popular.\n- **1984:** The EEC ==agreed== to give the UK an **annual rebate** (money back).\n- She also ==supported== **Lech Wałęsa** and the **Polish movement for freedom**.\n- Her policies ==reduced== inflation but caused **high unemployment** (about 3 million in 1982).","x":-2230,"y":-530,"width":550,"height":460},
		{"id":"1d647e218873680a","type":"text","text":"- In **1987**, Margaret Thatcher ==**won**== her **third election** with a big majority. Inflation and strikes ==**had almost disappeared**==, and people **were enjoying** tax cuts.\n- Later, prices ==started== to rise again, so she ==**had to increase**== interest rates.\n- She ==**opposed**== a single European currency and ==kept== asking South Africa to free Nelson Mandela.\n- In **1990**, she ==**sent**== British troops to help the U.S. after Iraq ==**had invaded**== Kuwait.\n- At home, her ==poll== tax was very unpopular and ==caused== riots.\n- After losing support, she ==resigned== in **November 1990**, and **John Major** became** Prime Minister.","x":-1136,"y":-716,"width":553,"height":336},
		{"id":"f428b864b1ab6e44","type":"text","text":"- **1983:** Margaret Thatcher ==won== the election with a **144-seat majority**. Inflation ==was== low and there were fewer strikes, but **unemployment ==stayed== high** (3.2 million).\n- **1984–1985:** The **miners’ strike** ==began== after plans to close uneconomic mines; it ==lasted== **one year**. The government ==demanded== **secret ballots** before strikes.\n- **1986:** A long **printers’ strike** started when Rupert Murdoch introduced new technology. It ended union control (“closed shops”) by 1990.\n- **1980s:** Thatcher ==focused== on **privatisation** — selling state companies like **British Telecom, British Gas, British Airways, and Rolls-Royce** — and **reducing government control**.\n- She ==abolished== local councils like the **Greater London Council** to cut costs and improve efficiency.\n- **Internationally**, she ==worked== with **Reagan and Gorbachev** during the Cold War, **supported the U.S.** in actions against **Libya and Grenada**, and in **1986**, agreed with **France** to build the **Channel Tunnel**.\n- She also ==signed== in **1984 a agreement with China** for the **return of Hong Kong in 1997**.","x":-1721,"y":-1144,"width":560,"height":520},
		{"id":"c797082a2fe8c30f","type":"text","text":"During World War 2 ","x":-341,"y":-293,"width":184,"height":50},
		{"id":"906a9238066314f2","type":"text","text":"The head of the government ==had been== Sir Wiston churhill (was a conservative)","x":-342,"y":-716,"width":180,"height":150},
		{"id":"31d981e24cfd20d5","x":612,"y":-904,"width":158,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"After the war"},
		{"id":"5d0ec33fcebea6ff","type":"text","text":"In 1945: Labour party ==won== the election ","x":525,"y":-746,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"fd1d842be3f98abd","type":"text","text":"Clement Atlee ==becam== the Prime minister ","x":235,"y":-796,"width":250,"height":50}
	],
